RudeSocket NEWS - User visible changes.
See ChangeLog for more detailed information on internal changes
See the end for copying conditions.

_______________________________________________________________________________
Release 1.7.1 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	- FIXED: destroying a Socket did not close its connection.  Nothing in
	  the teardown path closed anything - ~Socket deletes the
	  implementation, ~Socket_TCPClient deletes the connection and comm
	  objects, and Socket_Comm's destructor deliberately does not close
	  (its own comment says children should call finish(), and finish()
	  only unbinds).  So every Socket that went out of scope without an
	  explicit close() leaked its descriptor, and the peer was left holding
	  a connection that went away only when the process did.  A program
	  opening sockets in a loop ran out of descriptors: the regression test
	  counts 12 leaked across 12 sockets before the fix, none after.
	  The destructor now gives the communication layer its chance to shut
	  down cleanly - for TLS that is the close_notify - and then releases
	  the descriptor.
	- CHANGED: close() succeeds when there is nothing to close, instead of
	  reporting an error.  Closing twice, or closing a Socket that was
	  never connected, previously returned false; the second case said
	  "does not have a Socket_Comm to close", which was true and not a
	  problem.  This has to succeed now that the destructor closes, or the
	  ordinary "close() and then let it go out of scope" pattern would
	  either double close a descriptor or report a failure that is not one.
	- close() also releases the descriptor when the shutdown handshake
	  fails, rather than reporting the failure and leaving it open.

_______________________________________________________________________________
Release 1.7.0 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	- NEW: Socket::startSSL() negotiates TLS over a connection that is
	  already open.  This is the "STARTTLS" pattern, and it is what SMTP,
	  IMAP, POP3 and FTP need: those protocols open in the clear, read a
	  greeting, ask for encryption with a command, and only then upgrade.
	  connectSSL() cannot serve them, because it establishes TLS while
	  connecting - by the time the protocol knows it wants encryption, the
	  connection exists and the plaintext greeting has been read.  Until
	  now rudesocket could only offer TLS at connect time, so those
	  protocols could not be secured through it at all.
	- The descriptor is not disturbed by an upgrade; only the object
	  communicating over it is replaced.  The certificate is verified
	  against the host passed to connect(), honouring setSSLVerify()
	  exactly as connectSSL() does - and if you connected through a proxy,
	  that is still the destination host, which is who the TLS session is
	  with.
	- A failed handshake CLOSES the connection.  A half-negotiated session
	  is not usable, and the caller has no way to shut it down: close()
	  works through the communication object, which a failed bind leaves
	  unbound, and nothing in the teardown path closes the descriptor.
	  Do not call close() after startSSL() fails, and do not retry on the
	  same object.
	- Upgrading a connection that is already secure is refused rather than
	  nesting one TLS session inside another.
	- Without an OpenSSL build, startSSL() returns false and getError()
	  says so, matching connectSSL().

_______________________________________________________________________________
Release 1.6.0 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	- FIXED: setTimeout() had no effect on connect() under Windows.  It is
	  documented to cover "Connect, Read and Send", and covered two of the
	  three: the non-blocking machinery in connecttimeout() sat inside
	  #ifndef WIN32, so what ran on Windows was a plain blocking
	  ::connect() with the timeout ignored.  Connecting to an address that
	  silently drops packets waited out the stack's own SYN-retry period,
	  around 21 seconds, whatever the caller had asked for.  Winsock does
	  the same job under different names - ioctlsocket(FIONBIO) rather
	  than fcntl(O_NONBLOCK), WSAGetLastError() rather than errno - and
	  those differences are now confined to a few helpers, with the logic
	  shared.  Note that Windows reports a failed connect through
	  select()'s exception set rather than its write set, so both are
	  watched; without that a refused connection would look like a
	  timeout.
	- FIXED: with a timeout configured, a failed connect() described
	  itself as "Operation now in progress" no matter what actually went
	  wrong.  connecttimeout() switches the descriptor to non-blocking, so
	  ::connect() returns EINPROGRESS straight away and the real outcome
	  arrives later in the socket's SO_ERROR.  That value was read, tested
	  against zero and discarded, so errno still held EINPROGRESS by the
	  time the caller reached getError().  A refused connection, an
	  unreachable host and a reset all produced the same useless message -
	  and only when a timeout was set, since the untimed path reported
	  correctly.  SO_ERROR is now published through errno, and errno is
	  preserved across the call that restores the descriptor's mode.
	- FIXED: a failed connect() leaked the descriptor it had opened, and
	  nothing could reclaim it: close() declines to act unless the comm
	  object was bound, which only happens on success.  A caller that
	  retries ran out of descriptors.  simpleConnect() now closes what it
	  opened on every failure path, including a failed name resolution.
	- FIXED: on Windows, connect() errors were reported with
	  strerror(errno).  Winsock does not set errno - failures land in
	  WSAGetLastError() - so the message described whatever unrelated CRT
	  call had last touched errno.  Windows now formats the actual socket
	  error.

_______________________________________________________________________________
Release 1.5.0 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	- REMOVED: the autotools build (configure, configure.ac, Makefile.am,
	  Makefile.in, aclocal.m4, config.guess, config.sub, install-sh,
	  ltmain.sh, missing, depcomp and INSTALL).  It has been deprecated
	  since the CMake build landed, and the config.guess it shipped dated
	  from 2003, so it could not configure on any ARM machine - today that
	  means every Apple Silicon Mac and every ARM Linux server.  Its
	  install-sh was also committed without an executable bit, so
	  "make install" failed on macOS.  Check out v1.4.1 or earlier if you
	  still need it.
	- Consumers using FetchContent no longer inherit this project's demo
	  and test_* targets.  Tests and examples are now added only when this
	  is the top-level project.
	- The FetchContent example in README.md now pins GIT_TAG to v1.4.1
	  rather than tracking master.
	- The plain README no longer points at the removed INSTALL file or at
	  rudeserver.com addresses that stopped resolving years ago; bug
	  reports go to GitHub issues.
	- Removed the Borland bcc32 build instructions from README.  CMake
	  builds on Windows and is covered by CI; the hand-rolled bcc32
	  recipe had not been exercised in over a decade.

_______________________________________________________________________________
Release 1.4.1 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	- setTimeout() now bounds the SSL handshake, not just the reads and
	  sends that follow it.  A peer that accepted the TCP connection and
	  then never spoke TLS - a plaintext service on an https port, a
	  half-open load balancer - left connectSSL() blocked in the kernel
	  indefinitely, whatever timeout had been set.
	- pkg-config consumers can now detect SSL support: rudesocket.pc
	  defines RUDESOCKET_WITH_SSL in Cflags when the library was built
	  with OpenSSL, matching what the exported CMake target already did.
	- README now shows "pkg-config --static" in the compile line.  The
	  library is static by default and OpenSSL is a private dependency,
	  so the previous non-static command omitted -lssl/-lcrypto and the
	  link failed with undefined symbols.

_______________________________________________________________________________
Release 1.4.0 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	- BEHAVIOR CHANGE: readline() and reads() now return NULL at end of
	  stream (see below).  They previously returned an empty, non-NULL
	  string.  The header always documented NULL, so this restores the
	  documented contract - but code written against what the functions
	  actually did, rather than what they promised, will now see NULL
	  where it used to see "".  Released as 1.4.0 rather than 1.3.1 for
	  that reason.
	- Fixed setTimeout() being ignored on SSL reads.  1.3.0's notes below
	  claimed this was already fixed; it was not.  The socket is blocking
	  once connect() returns, so select()-then-SSL_read() was not enough:
	  a post-handshake record such as a TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket made the
	  socket readable, SSL_read() consumed it, found no application data
	  behind it, and then blocked in the kernel indefinitely.  Reads and
	  sends now run non-blocking for the duration of a timed call and
	  enforce a single deadline across the whole call.
	- Fixed readline() and reads() never reporting end of stream.  Both
	  returned an empty, non-NULL string once the peer had closed, and
	  kept returning it, so the documented loop
	
	      while((line = socket.readline()) != NULL)
	
	  never terminated - it span at full speed instead.  EOF with nothing
	  buffered is now NULL; a final unterminated line is still delivered
	  first.
	- Fixed pkg-config file: it hardcoded the prefix given at configure
	  time, so installing with "cmake --install --prefix <dir>" produced
	  a rudesocket.pc still pointing at the configure-time prefix (by
	  default /usr/local).  The prefix is now relative to ${pcfiledir}.
	- Added regression tests for the SSL read timeout and for EOF
	  handling.  Without these fixes the first hangs until the ctest
	  timeout kills it and the second spins.

_______________________________________________________________________________
Release 1.3.0 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	- BEHAVIOR CHANGE: connectSSL() now verifies the server's certificate
	  by default (system CA store + hostname check) and fails otherwise.
	  Call the new Socket::setSSLVerify(false) to accept self-signed or
	  mismatched certificates.
	- TLS repair: send SNI (modern servers rejected the handshake without
	  it), use the OpenSSL >= 1.1 API (TLS_client_method), consult
	  SSL_pending() so readline() over SSL no longer deadlocks, honor
	  setTimeout() on SSL reads and sends, and report SSL errors through
	  getError() instead of printing to stderr.
	  CORRECTION: the setTimeout() part of that claim was wrong - SSL
	  reads still blocked past the deadline.  Fixed in 1.4.0.
	- New CMake build system (autotools files retained but deprecated);
	  installs CMake package config and pkg-config files. SSL support is
	  optional: -DRUDESOCKET_WITH_SSL=OFF builds without OpenSSL, and
	  connectSSL() then fails gracefully with a clear error message
	  (previously it silently fell back to an UNENCRYPTED connection).
	- Code modernized to compile cleanly as C++17 with -Wall -Wextra
	  (sprintf -> snprintf, unused parameters, Windows header ordering).
	- Added test suite (ctest: echo, timeout, and SSL tests against a
	  local openssl s_server) and GitHub Actions CI covering Linux
	  x86_64/ARM, macOS, and Windows, plus an ASan/UBSan job.
	- New README with verified quick-start examples.

